[interviewer · INTRO] Your client is Brew & Bean, a specialty coffee chain with 200 locations across the US. Over the past two years, their net profit margin has declined from 24% to 6%, even though revenues have grown 15% over the same period. The CEO has hired us to identify the root cause of this profit decline and recommend how to reverse it. How would you approach this problem?

Take a moment, then walk me through how you'd approach this.

[candidate · 1:21] Okay, so profit is revenue minus cost, and since revenue grew but margin fell, something on the cost side is moving. Before I frame it, could I get a few data points?

One, the full cost breakdown by line item for both years. Two, revenue split by store versus new store openings. Three, average ticket size. Four, transaction volume per store. Five, the number of stores opened in the last two years. Six, employee headcount and wage rates. Seven, coffee bean commodity prices over the period. Eight, customer satisfaction scores and the Net Promoter Score trend.

That should give me enough to start narrowing down where the leakage is.
